<h1>About</h1>
<p class="lede">I row on the Lower Passaic most mornings, and nobody could tell me what was in the water we row on. So I started testing it.</p>

<h2>What this is</h2>
<p>A weekly E. coli reading at the Nereid Boat Club dock in Rutherford, New Jersey, published with the method beside it and scored against the published EPA criteria as one word: green, yellow or red. The readings are an open dataset anyone can download and check. The season began 23 August 2026 and is in progress.</p>

<h2>Who</h2>
<p>Haolin Li, Rutherford High School. I row out of the Nereid Boat Club and take every sample myself. The project is currently self-designed and unmentored: the protocol is built against published methods and criteria, and it has not yet been reviewed by anyone with credentials. If that changes, this page will say so by name &mdash; and until someone has agreed in writing, no name appears here.</p>

<h2>What this is not</h2>
<p>It is not a characterization of the Passaic River, which would take more stations, more years and better instruments than one high school student has. It is not advice about whether anyone should row; that call belongs to the people who make it, and this site only says what the water tested at. And it is not a finished season &mdash; the record is young, its known weaknesses are listed on the <a class='tx' href='/methods/'>methods page</a>, and the honest thing is to let it grow in public rather than dress it up.</p>

<h2>Contact</h2>
<p>Corrections, questions, or a better way to do any of this: <a class="tx" href="mailto:{{ site.email }}">{{ site.email }}</a>. A reviewer who improves the method is worth more than one who praises it.</p>
